---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PortNumberLookup from '@/components/tools/reference/PortNumberLookup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="port-number-lookup">
  <PortNumberLookup client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the port number lookup</h2>
    <p>
      Type a port number to see every service registered for it, or type a service name such as
      <code>ssh</code> or <code>postgresql</code> to find its port. A search for words, for example
      <code>remote desktop</code>, also looks in the descriptions. Each entry shows the transport
      protocols (TCP, UDP, SCTP, DCCP) and the RFC that defines it, when there is one.
    </p>
    <h2>The three port ranges</h2>
    <p>
      Port numbers go from 0 to 65535. Ports 0 to 1023 are system ports: on most systems only an
      administrator can open them, and they carry core services such as HTTP (80), HTTPS (443) and
      SSH (22). Ports 1024 to 49151 are registered ports that IANA assigns on request. Ports 49152 to
      65535 are dynamic ports that the operating system gives to the client side of a connection.
    </p>
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>
      The IANA Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number Registry is the official list. The site
      downloads it each month. A registration says which service asked for a port; it does not stop
      another program from using it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
